<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zondo Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2018">2018</a>–<a href="/years/2022">2022</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Judicial Commission of Inquiry into Allegations of State Capture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zondo Commission, formally the Judicial Commission of Inquiry into Allegations of State Capture, was a South African inquiry chaired by Deputy Chief Justice Raymond Zondo from 2018 to 2022. It investigated how business associates of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a>, notably the Gupta family, had influenced appointments and state contracts. Its reports found widespread corruption in state enterprises and government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 2016 the Public Protector, Thuli Madonsela, investigated state capture after complaints from Stanislaus Muyebe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a> of Southern Africa, another member of the public and the opposition leader Mmusi Maimane. Her report, State of Capture, published in November 2016, implicated Zuma and other officials in improper relationships with the Gupta family and recommended a commission of inquiry. Zuma asked the High Court to overturn the findings, but the court held the recommendation binding, and Chief Justice Mogoeng Mogoeng chose the chairperson. Former Auditor-General Terence Nombembe headed the investigations team.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The commission was set up in January 2018 but held its first hearing only in August 2018, after Zuma had resigned and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-ramaphosa">Cyril Ramaphosa</a> had replaced him. Over 300 witnesses testified. Vytjie Mentor said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> had offered to make her Minister of Public Enterprises in 2010; former finance minister Nhlanhla Nene described pressure over a R1.6-trillion nuclear deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; Angelo Agrizzi, former chief of operations of Bosasa, testified that the company bribed officials for contracts; members of the State Security Agency described its funds used for political ends.</p><p>Zuma testified from 16 July 2019, alleging a foreign-backed conspiracy against him, and later walked out of the commission without permission. After he defied a Constitutional Court order and refused to testify on 4 February 2021, the court found him in contempt and on 29 June 2021 sentenced him to 15 months in prison. Shortly after he was imprisoned on 7 July, unrest and looting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> from 9 to 17 July. The hearings closed in August 2021 with testimony from Ramaphosa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After eight extensions the report appeared in five parts between 4 January and 22 June 2022, at a cost to the state of close to R1 billion, far more than any earlier South African judicial inquiry. It found that state capture had taken place in government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and state-owned enterprises such as South African Airways, the South African Revenue Service and Transnet, and recommended prosecutions, Zuma's among them. Researchers at North-West University Business School found that its impact was limited by a lack of prosecutorial power and of political will to carry out its recommendations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zondo Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…constitutional democracy with regular elections, a free press and an independent judiciary, but it has struggled with corruption, notably the ‘state capture’ under Jacob Zuma investigated by the <b>Zondo Commission</b>, with the police killing of 34 striking miners at Marikana in 2012, and with crime and slow growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
